--TEST--
A typed capture is converted from the segment as it arrived, so `%34` is not a `uint` route's `4` — the conversion runs before the decode, and the two cannot be swapped — `rule:routing/matched-once-before-the-handler` and `rule:routing/a-capture-narrows-to-a-closed-set`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 5's capture narrows to the type its route parameter declared, and § 1 has
// the server compute those typed parameters at the door. That fixes an order:
// the conversion reads the raw segment, and the decode happens one layer above
// it, where the value crosses into the program. Were it the other way round,
// `/count/%34` would reach a `{n: uint}` route as `4` -- an escape choosing a
// row -- which is why the order is asserted here rather than left to be read
// off the two implementations.
class Counter
{
    #[Core\Route(path:"/count/{n}", method: Core\Http\Method::Get, name: "Counter::show")]
    #[Core\Access(allow: Core\Audience::Public)]
    public function show(uint $n): string
    {
        return "count " . $n;
    }
}

// The digits convert, and the value is the number rather than its text.
?Core\Router\Match $counted = Core\Router::match(Core\Http\Method::Get, "/count/34");
if ($counted != null) {
    uint $n = $counted->param("n") as uint;
    uint $next = $n + 1;
    echo "n + 1 = ", $next as string, "\n";
}

// `%34` is the escape for `4`. It reaches the conversion undecoded, is not a
// run of digits, and so claims nothing -- the row is not matched at all, rather
// than matched with a smuggled value.
?Core\Router\Match $escaped = Core\Router::match(Core\Http\Method::Get, "/count/%34");
if ($escaped == null) {
    echo "an escaped digit matches nothing", "\n";
}

// The ordinary failure to convert, for the same reason and by the same route:
// a segment the declared type cannot hold is not a match.
?Core\Router\Match $letters = Core\Router::match(Core\Http\Method::Get, "/count/abc");
if ($letters == null) {
    echo "a non-numeric segment matches nothing", "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
n + 1 = 35
an escaped digit matches nothing
a non-numeric segment matches nothing
